p.src:
Rotational invariance gives the same result for the next pair of directions: $P(B,\neg C)
  =
  \frac14\left(1-\frac1{\sqrt2}\right).$ For $A\wedge\neg C$, the second axis is the first spatial axis, so $P_{A\wedge\neg C}
  =
  \frac{I+\sigma_3}{2}\frac{I+\tau_1}{2}.$ The first factor again leaves $\ket{ud}/\sqrt2$. The $\tau_1$ term produces $\ket{uu}$, which is orthogonal to the singlet bra, while the identity term survives. Hence $P(A,\neg C)=\frac14.$ Combining the three results, $\underbrace{
  P(A,\neg B)+P(B,\neg C)
  }_{\frac12(1-1/\sqrt2)\approx0.146}
  <
  \underbrace{
  P(A,\neg C)
  }_{\frac14=0.25}.$

